"""Original social-finance and cooperative-governance reasoning."""


def build(c, m):
    return c(81, 'Social Finance Cooperative Models and Inclusion', 'Social Finance\nCooperative Models\nand Inclusion',
        'Compare fictional finance and member-governance arrangements for obligations, control, access and mission, without soliciting funds or advising investments.', [
        m('Compare finance terms and mission implications', 'Distinguish trading receipts, grants, debt and ownership contributions in an invented proposal.', [
            'Finance provides resources with different obligations and decision consequences. A trading receipt pays for an agreed offer; a grant may restrict use; debt normally creates repayment duties; an ownership contribution can affect control and expectations. These broad distinctions help read the invented term sheets, but they are not a description of every legal arrangement. General enterprise planning asks a team to connect funding needs to a coherent operating plan. Record the purpose, amount, timing, conditions and responsible decision role before treating a resource as available to support the mission. [S61]',
            'Read conditions as part of the proposal rather than a footnote. An invented grant might fund equipment but not recurring support, while a loan repayment may fall due before customers pay. An investor might seek a change in the intended market. Explain what each condition would mean for service access, cash and authority. Do not compare sources solely by the amount offered or assume a generous label means no obligations. Real agreements need qualified legal and financial review; coursework does not authorise borrowing, an investment recommendation or a request for funds from classmates. [S61]',
            'Research about financing can inform a question without selecting a source for an individual venture. A Slovenian observational study links organisational characteristics and financing to the adoption of impact measurement. Its outcome is a reporting practice, not measured beneficiary benefit or a return on investment. Therefore it cannot show that debt is always harmful or that a grant guarantees a mission outcome. In the fictional comparison, use the actual supplied terms and identify what remains uncertain. An association in a contextual sample does not replace analysis of repayment, control, restrictions and implementation. [R73]',
            'Write a finance comparison using common headings and a conditional conclusion. Identify which commitments must be met, which costs remain unfunded and which role can approve any real arrangement. A partnership can bring resources while adding dependence or competing demands, as qualitative evidence illustrates in a limited setting. State a concern that would require modification or a pause, and retain a record of assumptions. The learner output should improve scrutiny of a fictional proposal, rather than promote a financial product or promise that social purpose makes money risk-free. [R74]'],
            'An invented venture receives an equipment-only grant offer and a loan with payments due before customer receipts. Its preferred investor also wants to remove the supported-work objective. The draft ranks offers only by headline amount.',
            'Compare all three offers under purpose, timing, obligations, control and mission. Identify a condition requiring qualified review and one unfunded operating need.',
            'More money does not automatically mean a better fit. Restrictions, repayment timing, authority and mission consequences must remain visible.', ['S61', 'R73', 'R74'], [
                ('Finance sources create different conditions.', 'What must the fictional finance note record beyond amount?', 'Purpose, timing, obligations and control implications.', ['Only the largest headline.', 'A guaranteed return.', 'An assumption that social funding has no conditions.'], 'The terms determine how resources can support the operating plan.'),
                ('Restricted funds cannot be assumed available for every cost.', 'An invented grant covers equipment only. What remains unresolved?', 'Funding for recurring costs outside that restriction.', ['All future wages are automatically funded.', 'The restriction may be ignored.', 'Equipment pays every bill.'], 'A restricted resource does not cover unspecified operating needs.'),
                ('Repayment timing matters independently of expected sales.', 'A fictional loan payment precedes customer receipts. What should be examined?', 'The cash timing gap and a feasible authorised response.', ['Future revenue counted as cash today.', 'A guarantee that borrowing solves all gaps.', 'No repayment schedule.'], 'Expected income does not remove a present obligation.'),
                ('Measurement adoption is not investment performance.', 'What does the Slovenian study not establish for this learner?', 'Which financing source guarantees the best social and financial return.', ['A contextual association about reporting adoption.', 'A reason to inspect methods and dates.', 'The need for bounded interpretation.'], 'The design and measured outcome cannot support a universal financing prescription.'),
                ('Mission-changing terms need explicit scrutiny.', 'An invented investor wants to remove supported work. What should happen?', 'Review the condition against mission and authorised decision arrangements.', ['Accept silently because the amount is large.', 'Erase the original mission record.', 'Let coursework approve a real investment.'], 'The proposed condition changes the venture purpose and requires accountable review.')]),
        m('Examine member ownership and accountability', 'Explain how a fictional cooperative proposal could protect meaningful member control.', [
            'A cooperative identity concerns voluntary association, shared ownership and democratic control, rather than merely a community-oriented brand. The ICA statement provides a comparator for examining membership and decisions. In the invented venture, ask who can join, what membership entails, how representatives are held accountable and how members understand financial information. Do not infer a registered legal form from the name. A discussion about cooperative values is distinct from verifying Nigerian registration, tax, employment or regulated financial-service requirements, which the course does not determine or replace. [S63]',
            'Separate the roles of member, elected representative and paid manager. Each may have different responsibilities, and a functioning arrangement needs a clear way to explain decisions and address conflicts. A member vote is difficult to interpret if the relevant terms were inaccessible or alternatives were hidden. Describe an understandable fictional decision note showing the issue, options, proposed consequence and route for questions. Do not assume attendance means informed agreement, or that a majority preference removes the need to consider people affected by an exclusionary arrangement. These questions help examine governance quality within the supplied case. [S63]',
            'External resources can create a tension with member autonomy. If an invented funder claims the right to replace elected decision makers or redirect the service, examine that term instead of assuming capital and control can never conflict. Qualitative partnership research suggests useful questions about competing institutional goals, while remaining contextual and non-causal. Identify which decision belongs to members, which requires qualified review and what commitment would be inconsistent with the stated identity. The learner should not draft real bylaws or claim that a classroom vote binds a registered body. [R74]',
            'Prepare an original accountability note with decision owner, information required, explanation route and review point. Include a way for a relevant minority concern to remain visible after a decision. Record whether the supplied case demonstrates the arrangement operating or only proposes it. A cooperative label cannot guarantee fairness, profitability or protection against loss. The educational task is to reason about meaningful participation and control using invented facts, while leaving real constitutional documents, registration and financial obligations to appropriately qualified and authorised processes. [S63]'],
            'An invented cooperative holds a vote on unreadable terms. A funder may replace the elected group, and minority concerns about access disappear from the minutes. The team treats attendance as informed approval.',
            'Separate member, representative and manager roles. Propose four corrections to the decision information and accountability record without drafting legal bylaws.',
            'Usable information and actual member control matter. Record concerns and scrutinise the external-control term; a meeting count does not establish informed agreement.', ['S63', 'R74'], [
                ('A name does not establish cooperative status.', 'What does calling the fictional venture a cooperative fail to demonstrate?', 'Registered status and functioning member control.', ['A stated identity to examine.', 'A possible governance question.', 'A reason to clarify membership.'], 'Labels do not establish legal or operating arrangements.'),
                ('Governance roles should be distinguishable.', 'Why separate members, representatives and managers in the invented note?', 'Their decision and accountability responsibilities may differ.', ['They always have identical powers.', 'Managers automatically own every member vote.', 'Members need no information.'], 'Clear roles help identify who decides and who explains.'),
                ('Attendance is not informed agreement.', 'An invented vote uses unreadable terms. What is missing?', 'Understandable information about options and consequences.', ['Proof that every attendee consented.', 'A reason to erase questions.', 'An automatic guarantee of fairness.'], 'Participation counts do not establish understanding.'),
                ('External capital can affect autonomy.', 'A fictional funder can replace elected decision makers. What needs scrutiny?', 'Its compatibility with member control and authorised arrangements.', ['Only the publicity value of the offer.', 'An assumption that all capital is neutral.', 'A claim that qualified review is unnecessary.'], 'A control term may change the cooperative identity in practice.'),
                ('Minority concerns should remain accurately recorded.', 'What improves the fictional decision minutes?', 'The decision, reasons and relevant unresolved access concerns.', ['Only the winning slogan.', 'Deletion of dissent as proof of unity.', 'Private real-world member files.'], 'An accountable record does not manufacture unanimous agreement.')]),
        m('Assess inclusion and financial-service boundaries', 'Identify who may be excluded by a fictional arrangement and recognise regulated-service limits.', [
            'Inclusion asks who can use an arrangement on workable terms, not merely who is named as eligible. In the invented member enterprise, a joining payment, meeting time, inaccessible information or irregular income may create practical barriers. Describe the task and supplied circumstance rather than stereotype a group. Cooperative values offer a reason to examine participation and benefit alongside member responsibilities. An accessible option still needs resources and a functioning response; lowering a fee without funding the service could create another barrier later. State the trade-off rather than promise that one change solves every exclusion. [S63]',
            'Financial inclusion should not be equated with taking on more debt. A person may need understandable terms, a reliable payment route or protection from an unsuitable obligation. This course uses fictional transactions and does not recommend a product or determine suitability. General planning questions can identify a repayment dependency or an unfunded service cost, while actual decisions require qualified advice. Do not invite learners to pool real savings, lend to classmates or collect deposits for an imagined cooperative. A social mission and a course certificate do not provide authority to offer regulated financial services. [S61]',
            'Compare an access adjustment against the stated mission and available resources. An invented instalment option could lower an entry barrier but change cash timing; a supported information session could help understanding but require staffing. Record who decides, what the option costs and how an affected perspective would be heard without personal disclosure. Impact guidance encourages stakeholder relevance and proportionate evidence. Use a small invented access record to examine the option, while acknowledging the people missing from the record and the limits on what the exercise can tell us about actual outcomes. [S64]',
            'Conclude with an inclusion review and a boundary check. Identify the barrier, a feasible conditional response, a responsible role and an observation for reconsideration. Contextual partnership evidence reminds us that outside support can add obligations as well as capacity. Ask whether the invented partner changes who controls the offer or who bears its cost. The final note should preserve mission, practical feasibility and authority limits together. It must not guarantee investment protection, authorise deposit-taking or treat an attendance list, a loan count or a member label as proof of equitable benefit. [R74]'],
            'An invented membership payment excludes a low-cash-income route. The team proposes instalments without checking timing, then asks learners to collect real deposits to demonstrate financial inclusion.',
            'Identify the barrier and an assumption behind instalments. Propose a fictional review measure and explain why collecting deposits is outside the course.',
            'Consider access and cash timing together using invented records. Real deposits, lending and financial-service permissions require separate verified authority and qualified processes.', ['S63', 'S61', 'S64', 'R74'], [
                ('Formal eligibility differs from practical access.', 'A fictional joining payment is unaffordable under the supplied circumstances. What is demonstrated?', 'A practical entry barrier to examine.', ['That every eligible person can join.', 'That exclusion is necessarily intentional.', 'That no resources are needed.'], 'A rule can appear open while its terms obstruct actual participation.'),
                ('More debt is not automatic financial inclusion.', 'Which fictional conclusion is sound?', 'Understandable suitable arrangements matter beyond a loan count.', ['Every additional loan guarantees benefit.', 'Debt removes every access barrier.', 'No terms need explanation.'], 'Access to an obligation and a useful inclusive outcome are distinct.'),
                ('An access adjustment can affect cash timing.', 'What must accompany the invented instalment proposal?', 'A check of receipts, service costs and decision responsibility.', ['An assumption that timing stays identical.', 'A promise of guaranteed returns.', 'Removal of the cash schedule.'], 'Changing payment timing can change operating feasibility.'),
                ('Coursework does not authorise regulated financial activity.', 'How should learners handle the proposed collection of real deposits?', 'Keep the exercise fictional and require separate verified authority for real services.', ['Collect money to prove commitment.', 'Treat a cooperative label as permission.', 'Promise deposit protection.'], 'Educational completion does not confer financial-service powers.'),
                ('Inclusion review needs an observation and boundary.', 'Which fictional review note is strongest?', 'Barrier, feasible response, responsible role, evidence and authority limits.', ['A member count labelled universal equity.', 'A fundraising appeal to classmates.', 'An unqualified investment guarantee.'], 'A bounded review connects the intended benefit to practical and authorised conditions.')])])
